</section>

<section id="sources">
<h2>Sources and further reading</h2>
<p>Measurements in this article were made on Redis 8.10.3 (a Homebrew build on macOS, arm64, using the libc memory allocator), with client and server on one machine. They are the <code>redis-cli</code> transcripts, the 0.095 ms median round trip, the hit ratios of a real Redis under paced Zipf-like traffic, the keyspace counters, the 96-byte key, and the fail-open timings of the read-path code (redis-py 8.1.0 on Python 3.14, against a stopped Redis and an address that never answers). The <code>redis-py</code> default timeout and retry policy are from its 8.1.0 source. Every other number comes from an illustrative model, described in each figure's caption.</p>
<ul>
  <li>Redis documentation: <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/commands/set/"><code>SET</code></a> (the <code>EX</code>, <code>PX</code> and <code>KEEPTTL</code> options), <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/commands/info/"><code>INFO</code></a> (<code>keyspace_hits</code> and <code>keyspace_misses</code>), <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/using-commands/keyspace/">the keyspace</a>, and <a href="https://redis.io/docs/latest/operate/oss_and_stack/management/optimization/benchmarks/">benchmarking</a>, which explains why a single client mostly measures the network and the client.</li>
  <li>Lee Breslau, Pei Cao, Li Fan, Graham Phillips and Scott Shenker, <a href="https://pages.cs.wisc.edu/~cao/papers/zipf-implications.html">“Web Caching and Zipf-like Distributions: Evidence and Implications”</a>, IEEE INFOCOM 1999. The source of the exponents 0.64 to 0.83 and the shares of documents and requests quoted above.</li>
  <li>Peter J. Denning, “The Working Set Model for Program Behavior”, Communications of the ACM 11(5), 1968.</li>
  <li>Nathan Bronson, Abutalib Aghayev, Aleksey Charapko and Timothy Zhu, <a href="https://sigops.org/s/conferences/hotos/2021/papers/hotos21-s11-bronson.pdf">“Metastable Failures in Distributed Systems”</a>, HotOS 2021. Section 2.2 describes how losing a look-aside cache with a 90% hit rate multiplies database queries tenfold and can keep the cache from refilling.</li>
  <li>Rajesh Nishtala and others, <a href="https://www.usenix.org/system/files/conference/nsdi13/nsdi13-final170_update.pdf">“Scaling Memcache at Facebook”</a>, NSDI 2013. A large deployment of the cache-aside pattern this article introduces.</li>
  <li>Microsoft Azure Architecture Center, <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/patterns/cache-aside">“Cache-Aside pattern”</a>.</li>
  <li>Managed defaults: <a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonElastiCache/latest/dg/ParameterGroups.Engine.html">Amazon ElastiCache parameters</a> and <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/azure-cache-for-redis/cache-configure">Azure Cache for Redis configuration</a> both give <code>volatile-lru</code> as the default <code>maxmemory-policy</code>.</li>
  <li>PostgreSQL documentation, <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/runtime-config-resource.html">resource consumption</a> (<code>shared_buffers</code>), and MySQL documentation, <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-buffer-pool.html">the InnoDB buffer pool</a>.</li>
  <li><a href="https://valkey.io/">Valkey</a>, the fork of Redis 7.2.</li>
  <li>redis-py 8.1.0 source: <a href="https://github.com/redis/redis-py/blob/v8.1.0/redis/_defaults.py"><code>_defaults.py</code></a> (socket timeouts of five seconds; ten retries with jittered exponential backoff from 10 ms, capped at one second), <a href="https://github.com/redis/redis-py/blob/v8.1.0/redis/client.py"><code>client.py</code></a> (the <code>Redis</code> constructor's default <code>retry</code>), <a href="https://github.com/redis/redis-py/blob/v8.1.0/redis/retry.py"><code>retry.py</code></a> (retries on connection errors and timeouts) and <a href="https://github.com/redis/redis-py/blob/v8.1.0/redis/exceptions.py"><code>exceptions.py</code></a>, where <code>ConnectionError</code> and <code>TimeoutError</code> are both kinds of <code>RedisError</code>.</li>
</ul>
